<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Java Sea</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> Sea is a shallow sea between Java, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. It was a key maritime trade route used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> empires. In 1942 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-java-sea">Battle of the Java Sea</a> was a major Japanese naval victory that sealed the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/java-sea.jpg" alt="Java Sea" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FarisJamal · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karangsong_beach_in_Indramayu,_West_Jawa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wijaya's political skill, the fertile rice plains of the Brantas valley and access to trade routes across the <b>Java Sea</b> gave the new state both agricultural and commercial resources.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sailendra dynasty, which appears to have been closely connected to Srivijaya from the eighth century, built the great Buddhist monument of Borobudur in central Java, which suggests that Srivijayan cultural and political influence reached across the <b>Java Sea</b> at the empire's height.</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Surabaya controlled the trade of the Brantas valley and the eastern <b>Java Sea</b>, with links to Banjarmasin, Sukadana and the spice islands.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-surabaya">Duchy of Surabaya</a></li><li><blockquote>Bugis seafarers from Sulawesi settled at Loloan near Negara in the 17th and 18th centuries, adding a Muslim trading community to the Hindu Balinese population and linking the kingdom to the shipping routes of the <b>Java Sea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-jembrana">Kingdom of Jembrana</a></li></ul></section>
